feat(spmi): write the PON bootloader restart reason through a gated RW channel

The FASTBOOT command wrote the Qualcomm restart cookie to IMEM, but the
next boot never saw it.  The vendor kernel also stores
PON_RESTART_REASON_BOOTLOADER in the PMIC PON block, and that register is
the one part of the path this kernel could only read.  Add the write half
and make FASTBOOT the single supported fastboot entry point:

  - map exactly one 4 KiB read/write channel slice at
    chnls + 0x10000*apid (0x0C6D0000 for the PON APID 0x00d), not the
    32 MiB window, which does not fit the 8 MiB io_map submap;
  - issue a single-byte PMIC_ARB_OP_EXT_WRITEL command and poll the
    channel status with the same bounded timeout and DONE/FAILURE/
    DENIED/DROPPED checks the observer path uses;
  - re-read the APID mapping and ownership registers before every write
    and refuse unless the APID still maps PPID 0x008 and write_ee is this
    EE (0), matching pmic_arb_offset_v5();
  - read SOFT_RB_SPARE 0x88F, print the old value, write
    (old & 0x01) | 0x04 under mask 0xFE (Gen2 layout, reason 0x02 << 1),
    read it back with bounded retries, and verify both the target field
    and the untouched bit 0.

Everything is behind kebab_pon_write=1, default off, and FASTBOOT now
gates both halves of the request together: the PON reason and the IMEM
cookie.  With the gate off it refuses and points at REBOOT, so a boot
that did not ask for fastboot never writes a PMIC register or an IMEM
cookie.  kebab_pon_verbose=1 optionally shows the raw arbiter traffic;
the normal output is a reason line, a verification line, the IMEM line
and the restart line.

The channel is mapped at start-up in kebab_spmi_init(), next to IMEM,
because the command can run from the panic loop where ml_io_map cannot
allocate.  PON? remains the read-only diagnostic.

